## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses foundational iOS skills: data modeling with Core Data, SwiftUI form bindings, basic validation, and a live-updating list with category totals. It mirrors real apps and allows quick evaluation of MVVM patterns.

## Key Concepts
- Core Data modeling
- SwiftUI MVVM bindings
- Simple input validation
- List with computed totals and search

## Code Example
```swift
import CoreData
@objc(Transaction)
public class Transaction: NSManagedObject {
  @NSManaged public var amount: NSDecimalNumber
  @NSManaged public var category: String?
  @NSManaged public var date: Date?
  @NSManaged public var note: String?
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you add unit tests for validation?
- How would you extend to grouping by date category or export data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests basic SwiftUI state management, on-device persistence, live UI updates, and accessibility considerations in a beginner-friendly setting.

## Key Concepts
- SwiftUI @AppStorage for lightweight persistence
- Color theming and dynamic UI updates
- Accessibility labeling and dynamic type considerations
- Basic test planning: persistence, accessibility, UI consistency

## Code Example
```swift
@AppStorage("themeColor") private var themeColorHex: String = "#4285F4"
@AppStorage("fontSize") private var fontSize: Double = 14.0

var body: some View {
  VStack {
    // color palette and font size slider UI here
    Text("Preview text").font(.system(size: CGFloat(fontSize)))
      .foregroundColor(Color(hex: themeColorHex))
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate edge cases like invalid saved color values?
- How would you extend this to support dark mode vs light mode themes?

